<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section">3  Asymptotic clustering</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geshkovski et al., (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geshkovski et al., (2025)</a></cite>, theoretical analyses of attention dynamics have primarily focused on establishing asymptotic clustering under the <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Post-LN</span> scheme, namely <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{j}(t)\to\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{j}(t)\to\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>, under a generic initialization; see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Criscitiello et al., (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., (2025)</a></cite>. However, empirical studies have revealed that in practice, tokens often remain trapped in metastable states for extended periods before clustering emerges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geshkovski et al., 2024a, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bruno et al., 2025a, </a>)</cite>. Despite this, the clustering phenomenon appears to occur at multiple local scales, and the simplified setting considered in prior work continues to offer valuable insights, as we will demonstrate in the next section. In this section, we extend the analysis and show that asymptotic clustering persists beyond the original <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Post-LN</span> framework to other normalization schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that we study the normalized attention dynamics (<a href="#S2.Ex5" title="In 2.3 Speed regulation formulation ‣ 2 Normalized Attention Dynamics ‣ Normalization in Attention Dynamics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">NA</span></a>) defined by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\theta}_{j}(t)=\frac{1}{s_{j}(t)}\,\mathbf{P}_{\theta_{j}(t)}\,A^{t}_{j}(\Theta(t))=\frac{1}{s_{j}(t)}\,\mathbf{P}_{\theta_{j}(t)}\,\sum_{k=1}^{n}V\theta_{k}(t)\frac{e^{\beta\langle Q\theta_{j}(t),K\theta_{k}(t)\rangle}}{\sum_{l=1}^{n}e^{\beta\langle Q\theta_{j}(t),K\theta_{l}(t)\rangle}}\,\quad j=1,\ldots,n\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.17em;" width="1.17em"></mspace><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\theta}_{j}(t)=\frac{1}{s_{j}(t)}\,\mathbf{P}_{\theta_{j}(t)}\,A^{t}_{j}(\Theta(t))=\frac{1}{s_{j}(t)}\,\mathbf{P}_{\theta_{j}(t)}\,\sum_{k=1}^{n}V\theta_{k}(t)\frac{e^{\beta\langle Q\theta_{j}(t),K\theta_{k}(t)\rangle}}{\sum_{l=1}^{n}e^{\beta\langle Q\theta_{j}(t),K\theta_{l}(t)\rangle}}\,\quad j=1,\ldots,n\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the speed regulation factor <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is given in Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2.3 Speed regulation formulation ‣ 2 Normalized Attention Dynamics ‣ Normalization in Attention Dynamics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. It is interesting to note that both <span id="S3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pre-LN</span> and <span id="S3.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Peri-LN</span> are not directly regulated by an explicit mechanism but rather by the <em id="S3.p2.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">magnitude</em>. In particular, this mechanism dampens the speed of each token individually according to their magnitude.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The main observation of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geshkovski et al., (2025)</a></cite> is that when <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="KQ^{\top}=QK^{\top}=V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">KQ^{\top}=QK^{\top}=V</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Post-LN</span> system is a gradient flow for the energy function</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E(\Theta):=-\sum_{j,k=1}^{n}e^{\beta\langle Q\theta_{k},K\theta_{j}\rangle}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\Theta):=-\sum_{j,k=1}^{n}e^{\beta\langle Q\theta_{k},K\theta_{j}\rangle}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where we recall that <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta=[\theta_{1},\ldots,\theta_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta=[\theta_{1},\ldots,\theta_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For (<a href="#S2.Ex5" title="In 2.3 Speed regulation formulation ‣ 2 Normalized Attention Dynamics ‣ Normalization in Attention Dynamics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">NA</span></a>), we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\theta}_{j}(t)=-\frac{1}{s_{j}(t)Z_{j}(t)}\nabla_{\theta_{j}}E(\Theta(t))\,,\quad\text{where}\ Z_{j}(t)=\sum_{l=1}^{n}e^{\beta\langle Q\theta_{j}(t),K\theta_{l}(t)\rangle}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\theta}_{j}(t)=-\frac{1}{s_{j}(t)Z_{j}(t)}\nabla_{\theta_{j}}E(\Theta(t))\,,\quad\text{where}\ Z_{j}(t)=\sum_{l=1}^{n}e^{\beta\langle Q\theta_{j}(t),K\theta_{l}(t)\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∇</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the spherical (Riemannian) gradient.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The above dynamics can be seen as modulated gradient flow, albeit with a complicated modulator that depends on time and space. For vanilla gradient flows, that is for <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}(t)Z_{j}(t)=\textrm{const.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>const.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}(t)Z_{j}(t)=\textrm{const.}</annotation></semantics></math>, a celebrated result of Łojasiewicz guarantees convergence of this gradient flow to a critical point of the energy. Following the same steps, we show in the Appendix D.1 that this result extends to the present framework, guaranteeing convergence of any trajectory. From there, we establish the following clustering result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the normalized attention dynamics (<a href="#S2.Ex5" title="In 2.3 Speed regulation formulation ‣ 2 Normalized Attention Dynamics ‣ Normalization in Attention Dynamics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">NA</span></a>) with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=K=V=I_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=K=V=I_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for uniformly sampled initializations <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(0)\in({\mathcal{S}}^{d-1})^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(0)\in({\mathcal{S}}^{d-1})^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Post-LN</span>, <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">nGPT</span>, <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LN-Scaling</span> cluster asymptotically</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}[\{\textrm{tokens synchronize to 1 cluster}\}]=1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">tokens synchronize to 1 cluster</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[\{\textrm{tokens synchronize to 1 cluster}\}]=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">whereas for a standard Gaussian sample of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(0):=r(0)\cdot\Theta(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(0):=r(0)\cdot\Theta(0)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(0)\in({\mathcal{S}}^{d-1})^{\otimes n},r(0)\in\mathbb{R}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(0)\in({\mathcal{S}}^{d-1})^{\otimes n},r(0)\in\mathbb{R}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pre-LN</span>, <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mix-LN</span>, <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Peri-LN</span> one has</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}[\{\textrm{tokens $\theta_{j}$ synchronize to 1 cluster}\}\cup\{\min_{j\in[n]}\liminf_{t\to\infty}\dot{r}_{j}(t)=0\}]=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">tokens </mtext><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> synchronize to 1 cluster</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim inf</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[\{\textrm{tokens $\theta_{j}$ synchronize to 1 cluster}\}\cup\{\min_{j\in[n]}\liminf_{t\to\infty}\dot{r}_{j}(t)=0\}]=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, this result holds not only for <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{t}=K^{t}=V^{t}=I_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{t}=K^{t}=V^{t}=I_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> but more generally for <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{t}=Q,K^{t}=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{t}=Q,K^{t}=K</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{t}=V=Q^{\top}K=K^{\top}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{t}=V=Q^{\top}K=K^{\top}Q</annotation></semantics></math> as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sander et al., (2022)</a></cite>. The second condition on the magnitude growth can be traced with Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2.3 Speed regulation formulation ‣ 2 Normalized Attention Dynamics ‣ Normalization in Attention Dynamics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> definition to work with further. For example, we immediately get the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pre-LN</span>, <span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Peri-LN</span> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq e^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq e^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> we have unconditional synchronization.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This statement follows from a simple lower bound on <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{r}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{r}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We write it for <span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pre-LN</span>, and <span id="S3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Peri-LN</span> can be done similarly.</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{r}_{j}=\langle\theta_{j},A_{j}(\Theta)\rangle=\frac{1}{Z_{j}}(e^{\beta}\langle\theta_{j},\theta_{j}\rangle+\sum_{k\neq j}e^{\beta\langle\theta_{k},\theta_{j}\rangle}\langle\theta_{k},\theta_{j}\rangle)\geq\frac{1}{ne^{\beta}}(e^{\beta}-(n-1))\geq\frac{1}{ne^{\beta}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>β</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>β</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{r}_{j}=\langle\theta_{j},A_{j}(\Theta)\rangle=\frac{1}{Z_{j}}(e^{\beta}\langle\theta_{j},\theta_{j}\rangle+\sum_{k\neq j}e^{\beta\langle\theta_{k},\theta_{j}\rangle}\langle\theta_{k},\theta_{j}\rangle)\geq\frac{1}{ne^{\beta}}(e^{\beta}-(n-1))\geq\frac{1}{ne^{\beta}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where we used the fact that any negative term in the second sum is at most <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\beta}\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>β</mi></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\beta}\geq n</annotation></semantics></math> and a trivial bound on <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section">4  Initial and terminal token velocities</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The previous section established an asymptotic result but did not address the rate at which tokens cluster, an aspect that is crucial for understanding how representations evolve. This question is important because the velocity at time <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> determines the influence of the <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>th layer in shaping the final token representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Before analyzing the propagation speed of tokens in our attention dynamics model, we first discuss a benchmark for desirable behavior. In an efficient architecture, each layer should meaningfully transform token representations, causing substantial displacement in representation space. If tokens remain nearly stationary across many layers, the architecture risks <em id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">representation collapse</em>. Equally important, however, is ensuring that early layers contribute significantly—delaying transformation until later stages can limit the expressive power of the network.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">4.1  Prelude: Symmetric initialization</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geshkovski et al., (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cowsik et al., (2024)</a></cite>, we begin with a so-called orthogonal symmetric initialization where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\theta_{j}(0),\theta_{k}(0)\rangle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\theta_{j}(0),\theta_{k}(0)\rangle=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}(0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}(0)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. This configuration approximately matches that of randomly initialized tokens in high dimension. Due to the symmetry, the cosine similarity <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)=\langle\theta_{j}(t),\theta_{k}(t)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)=\langle\theta_{j}(t),\theta_{k}(t)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq k</annotation></semantics></math> and the entire token dynamics reduces to the evolution of two scalar quantities: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the Appendix, we derive a simple ODE for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t),r(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t),r(t)</annotation></semantics></math> following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geshkovski et al., (2025, Theorem 6.8)</a></cite>. We plot ODE-based evolution of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Prelude: Symmetric initialization ‣ 4 Initial and terminal token velocities ‣ Normalization in Attention Dynamics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> with parameters <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=256</annotation></semantics></math>. Despite its simplicity, this setup already provides striking insight into the effects of different normalization schemes. The importance tracks in how cosine similarity evolution is alike in the theoretical formula plotted in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Prelude: Symmetric initialization ‣ 4 Initial and terminal token velocities ‣ Normalization in Attention Dynamics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and the experimental setup with random weights modeled in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Prelude: Symmetric initialization ‣ 4 Initial and terminal token velocities ‣ Normalization in Attention Dynamics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the normalized attention dynamics (<a href="#S2.Ex5" title="In 2.3 Speed regulation formulation ‣ 2 Normalized Attention Dynamics ‣ Normalization in Attention Dynamics" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">NA</span></a>) with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=K=V=I_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=K=V=I_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> initialized at a symmetric orthogonal configuration, i.e. <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\theta_{j}(0),\theta_{k}(0)\rangle=\delta_{jk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\theta_{j}(0),\theta_{k}(0)\rangle=\delta_{jk}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}(0)=r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}(0)=r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the cosine similarity <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(t)=\langle\theta_{j}(t),\theta_{k}(t)\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(t)=\langle\theta_{j}(t),\theta_{k}(t)\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> remains constant across all pairs <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\gamma}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\gamma}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
